# Date and time scales: scale_date

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_x_date()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_date.html), which covers
`scale_y_date()`, `scale_x_datetime()`, `scale_y_datetime()`, `scale_x_time()` and
`scale_y_time()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and
then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

These are the position scales for dates, date-times and times of day, which ggplot2 adds by itself
for a column of one of them. Setting one by hand chooses its limits, where its breaks fall and how
its labels are written, in units of time such as `"1 week"` and formats such as `"%b %d"`. Giggly
has `Giggly.Plot.scale_x_date/2` and `Giggly.Plot.scale_y_date/2`, with their limits, breaks, labels
and minor breaks, breaks also as a step of time (`date_breaks: [week: 1]`) and labels as a
`Calendar.strftime/3` format (`date_labels: "%b %d"`); it has no `scale_x_datetime()` or
`scale_x_time()`, since date-times and times of day have no scale yet. A plot is described with
`Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell
aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and
shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`.

## Breaks and labels

`Sys.Date()` is today's date, so the data is the last 30 days, each with a random price.

```r
last_month <- Sys.Date() - 0:29
set.seed(1)
df <- data.frame(
  date = last_month,
  price = runif(30)
)
base <- ggplot(df, aes(date, price)) +
  geom_line()

# The date scale will attempt to pick sensible defaults for
# major and minor tick marks. Override with date_breaks, date_labels
# date_minor_breaks arguments.
base + scale_x_date(date_labels = "%b %d")
base + scale_x_date(date_breaks = "1 week", date_labels = "%W")
base + scale_x_date(date_minor_breaks = "1 day")
```

Giggly draws the first and third plots. The cell takes today's date from the clock, as
`Sys.Date()` does, though `Date.utc_today/0` gives the date in UTC where R gives the local one, and
writes out the 30 prices R's `runif()` draws after `set.seed(1)`. A step of time is written as
`Date.shift/2` takes one, `[day: 1]` for `"1 day"`, and a format is `Calendar.strftime/3`'s, which
writes `"%b %d"` as R does, `Oct 19`.

```elixir
today = Date.utc_today()

df = %{
  "date" => Enum.map(0..29, fn days -> Date.add(today, -days) end),
  "price" => [
    0.2655086631421, 0.37212389963679016, 0.5728533633518964, 0.9082077899947762,
    0.2016819310374558, 0.8983896849676967, 0.9446752686053514, 0.6607977924868464,
    0.6291140438988805, 0.061786270467564464, 0.20597457489930093, 0.17655675252899528,
    0.6870228466577828, 0.384103718213737, 0.7698414199985564, 0.49769924208521843,
    0.7176185082644224, 0.9919060948304832, 0.3800351794343442, 0.7774452213197947,
    0.9347052311059088, 0.21214252128265798, 0.651673766085878, 0.12555509596131742,
    0.26722066872753203, 0.3861140925437212, 0.013390333158895373, 0.3823879570700228,
    0.86969084572047, 0.3403489966876805
  ]
}

base =
  df
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :date, y: :price})
  |> Plot.geom_line()

base
|> Plot.scale_x_date(date_labels: "%b %d")
|> render.()
```

```elixir
base
|> Plot.scale_x_date(date_minor_breaks: [day: 1])
|> render.()
```

Not in Giggly yet: `"%W"` in `date_labels`, the week of the year, which `Calendar.strftime/3`
cannot write.

## Limits

```r
# Set limits
base + scale_x_date(limits = c(Sys.Date() - 7, NA))
```

R's `NA` leaves the upper limit to the data, today; Giggly writes it `nil`. The line through the
days before the limit is not drawn.

```elixir
base
|> Plot.scale_x_date(limits: {Date.add(today, -7), nil})
|> render.()
```
